 ## Overview
-Acousto-Optic Random Access Scanning with Parallel Channels is a volumetric display technique that uses multiple acousto-optic (AO) deflectors operating in parallel to address voxels in a display volume, thereby increasing the total voxel count and maintaining image intensity [S1, unofficial]. It solves the bottleneck of finite acoustic wave travel time ("access time") in single-channel scanners, which limits the number of addressable voxels per frame at a fixed refresh rate [S1, unofficial]. Unlike single-channel scanners, which suffer from intensity drop-off as voxel density increases due to reduced on-time, this method couples several scanners to distribute the workload [S1, unofficial]. It differs from [Four-Channel Scanner Convergence Correction](11734-four-channel-scanner-convergence-correction.md) by defining the parallel architecture itself, whereas the sibling method addresses the geometric alignment errors inherent in that architecture.
+Acousto-Optic Random Access Scanning with Parallel Channels is a volumetric display technique that uses multiple acousto-optic (AO) deflectors operating in parallel to address voxels in a display volume, thereby increasing the total voxel count and maintaining image intensity [S1, unofficial]. It solves the bottleneck of finite acoustic wave travel time ("access time") in single-channel scanners, which limits the number of addressable voxels per frame at a fixed refresh rate [S1, unofficial]. Unlike single-channel scanners, which suffer from intensity drop-off as voxel density increases due to reduced on-time, this method couples several scanners to distribute the workload [S1, unofficial]. It differs from [Four-Channel Scanner Convergence Correction](11734-four-channel-scanner-convergence-correction.md) by defining the parallel architecture itself, whereas the sibling method addresses the geometric alignment errors inherent in that architecture [S1, unofficial].
 
